@ 17.2.4 - Common Anatolian to Lydian
# citation: Alces, from http://www.unc.edu/~melchert/anathistphon.pdf (link is dead) “[P]lus lots of syncope which he doesn’t elaborate on”
	d > t͡ʃ / :{ _i, _u }:
	d > t / :{ #_, N_ }:
	{ p, d, D } > { f, ð, T }
	T > D / N_
	Ḱ > [+ cons, - fr, + bk, + hi, - lo]
	[+ cons, - fr, + bk, + hi, - lo]ʷ > [+ cons, - fr, + bk, + hi, - lo] / _V:[+round]
	ʃ > s
	s > ʃ / :{ _i, _e }:
	s > ʃ / i_
	{h,h} > ∅
	{m,n} > ʋ / _# (that’s a Greek nu, I’m not sure what it’s supposed to represent)
	l > ʎ / :{ _i, _j }:
	w ( > v?) / s_
	v?) > f / s_
	j > ∅ / C_
	{ j, u } > { ð, w } / #_
	j > ð / V_V
	e > i / j_
	{e,a,o} > e:[+long] / _N in :[+closed]
	{e,a,o} > a:[+long] / _N in :[+open]
	{e,o} > a / in :[-stress]
	n > ∅ / _P (leaves nasalization on the previous vowel)
	{ {, o,, o:[+long], }, e:[+long], æ:[+long] } > { {, a,, a:[+long], }, i:[+long], a:[+long] }
	V:[+long] > V:[-long]